Z||000000||Bruce||11-22-1999||09:58 PM||||I have a few drops of oil that appear on the drivers side, under the the general area of the transmission drain plug. I had a similar problem with a leak near the oil pan drain plug. In the latter case, I simply installed a new plug and gasket and problem solved. Does this seem like a similar case, or am I wishful thinking?<BR>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sage||11-22-1999||10:47 PM||||Best way I have found for finding leaks is to clean the suspect area and anything above  and or around the area very well. Then watch it and you should be able to see where the leak originates.  Plug and/or gasket would be a good place to start looking, but sometimes leaks are deceiving. Just clean everything really good and the leak will show itself to you. Preasure washer works good for this, but don't get your electrical stuff wet (at least not REAL wet!)  If you don't have one or know someone who has one, the car wash will work.  Get some engine degreaser and spray on before you go to car wash. Just be careful around sensors and obvious electrical connections and componants.||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||11-23-1999||08:22 AM||||That is exactly where I am now on my '84 SE<BR>except that I have gone one step further and<BR>drained the transaxle, taped up the drain<BR>plug with teflon (can't get a new one - it's<BR>not in the computer at advance auto) and<BR>refilled with 3 qts of 5W30. It's still <BR>leaking very slowly - it takes one or two<BR>days to form a big enough drop to drop onto<BR>the ground. It seems to be the transaxle, but<BR>I don't think it's the plug. I'll be<BR>glad to hear what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DISenchanted||11-23-1999||06:34 PM||||I just started having the exact same problem, except mine is leaking a little faster. (a drop a day)  I haven't had too much time to check it out, but it appears to be comming from the Axle itself.  I hope it's not as bad as it sounds.||||reg||1
